    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `sharesHousehold` / `householdId` behavior with minimal structural impact, reusing the existing household pipeline step rather than adding new classes or wiring.\n\n**What changed:**\n\n1. **`EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold.java`** \u2014 the step that already enforced household uniqueness and already honored `sharesHousehold=true`. I added two lines: it now computes a **stable, deterministic `householdId`** from the normalized `lastName`+`address` key (`Integer.toHexString(household.hashCode()).toUpperCase()`) and stores it on the owner *before* the opt-in short-circuit. Because it's derived purely from the household key, every owner in the same household (same last name + address) gets the identical id, satisfying the \"assign both the same householdId\" requirement \u2014 no cross-owner coordination or lookup needed.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** (model) \u2014 added a `householdId` field with getter/setter and `@Column(name = \"household_id\")`, mirroring the existing `customerCode`.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `householdId` as a read-only property on the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name (same as `customerCode`), returning it on `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n4. **Schema/data SQL** \u2014 added the `household_id` column to all four `schema.sql` files, and appended the trailing `NULL` to the positional owner seed inserts in the `hsqldb` and `mysql` `data.sql` (h2/postgres seeds use named columns, so they were unaffected).\n\nI kept the logic inside the one function that already owned this concern (its class has very little surrounding complexity), avoided a new function class and any new YAML step, and touched no other endpoint.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` passes: two same-household owners created with `sharesHousehold=true` are both accepted and report an identical, non-blank `householdId`.",
